\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $\mathbb H_{\mathbb Q}$ be the Hamilton quaternion algebra over $\mathbb Q$, and let $\mathcal O=\mathbb Z+i\mathbb Z+j\mathbb Z+k\mathbb Z$ be its Lipschitz order. Decide the solvability of arbitrary noncommutative polynomial equations with either rational or quaternion coefficients, and with unknowns constrained either to $\mathbb H_{\mathbb Q}$ or to $\mathcal O$. Also ask for existential definitions of $\mathbb Z$ in $\mathcal O$ and of $\mathbb Q$ in $\mathbb H_{\mathbb Q}$, and for the analogous decision questions in $M_n(\mathbb Z)$ and $M_n(\mathbb Q)$.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://aimath.org/WWN/hilberts10th/hilberts10th.pdf}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $\mathbb H_{\mathbb Q}$ be the Hamilton quaternion algebra over $\mathbb Q$, and let $\mathcal O=\mathbb Z+i\mathbb Z+j\mathbb Z+k\mathbb Z$ be its Lipschitz order. Decide the solvability of arbitrary noncommutative polynomial equations with either rational or quaternion coefficients, and with unknowns constrained either to $\mathbb H_{\mathbb Q}$ or to $\mathcal O$. Also ask for existential definitions of $\mathbb Z$ in $\mathcal O$ and of $\mathbb Q$ in $\mathbb H_{\mathbb Q}$, and for the analogous decision questions in $M_n(\mathbb Z)$ and $M_n(\mathbb Q)$.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 20002344 (AIM-LOGIC-0120). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://aimath.org/WWN/hilberts10th/hilberts10th.pdf}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:20002344}

